  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 256 PM EDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nAnomalous upper troughing continues its slow eastward progression \ninto the Ohio and Tennessee Valleys this afternoon. The associated \noccluded surface low is centered near the KY/IN/OH border, with an \naccompanying cold front advecting into eastern portions of the \nforecast area. Regional radar imagery depicts isolated to weakly \nscattered showers along this boundary locally. \n\nBetween model derived soundings and SPC mesoanalysis, this \nafternoons environment will be accompanied by MLCAPE 500-1250 J/kg, \nPWAT values around 1.5-1.8 inches, and effective shear near 30 kts. \nThe slightly drier air relative to the previous day will also \ncontribute to DCAPE 800-1100 J/kg across the northeast. Because of \nthis, a marginal risk for a isolated strong to damaging winds is in \nplace across the northeast. However, latest radar trends provide \ndecreasing confidence in this occurrence. Recent rains have also \nhelped saturate soils with many 1 hr ffgs in the 1.3-1.5 inch range, \nthus, isolated flooding could become a threat where slower moving or \ntraining storms occur along the boundary. \n\nA largely quiet night is expected, with the exception of patchy fog \nfor some. An upper low is expected to become gradually cutoff from \nmean flow as we head into the work week. This will promote daily \nchances for showers and maybe a few storms as the upper low churns \nover the southern Appalachians. Temperatures will also remain \nslightly below normal for early August. Because of worked over \nsoils, broad marginal risks remain in place for isolated flooding \npotential each day for the next several days. Some discrepancies \nremain with how/when the cutoff melds back into mean flow late week, \nbut latest trends suggest increasing influence of longwave troughing \nto our northwest will continue daily precip chances even as the low \nbecomes absorbed into mean flow sometime late week.",
